<!--
    GH-890. Stage P9, cast insertion (coreaction_casts.rs, get_input_cast_equal).

    An integer == or != on the bits of a float-typed value printed as a C
    float comparison: (v.u == 0x80000000u) printed a0 == -0.0, true for +0.0
    too; (v.u & 0x7fffffff) == 0x7fc00000 printed ABS(a0) == NAN, never true;
    and an == of two floats' bits printed a0 == a1, false for equal NaNs and
    true for -0.0 against 0.0. The ordered integer compares already
    reinterpreted the float. Now the equality compares the bits as an integer
    too, through the union reinterpretation, with the constant in hex, unless
    a float comparison is exact: against a normal number or an infinity whose
    literal C reads back as that value (the bits of 1.0f keep a0 == 1.0), or
    against +0.0 when the other side is an ABS (ABS(a0) == 0.0). A FLOAT_EQUAL
    or FLOAT_NOTEQUAL stays a == b.

    The XML wrapper only provides the harness architecture. The script loads
    floateq.c (source beside the fixtures) built by gcc -O2 and clang -O2 for
    x86-64, clang -O2 for AArch64 and clang -O2 for Cortex-M4F hard-float,
    whose doubles are soft-float and are not loaded.
-->
<bytechunk space="ram" offset="0x100000" readonly="true">
c3
</bytechunk>
<symbol space="ram" offset="0x100000" name="harness_stub"/>
</binaryimage>
